+ *   1. A hardware test device on their private LAN (e.g. 192.168.1.x)
+ *   2. A companion CLI running on their own machine (127.0.0.1)
+ *
+ * Security model (a public website talking to private/loopback hosts is a
+ * classic CSRF / DNS-rebinding / network-scanning vector, so we keep the
+ * surface deliberately small):
+ *   - Connections happen only on an explicit user click. No auto-connect, no
+ *     background polling, no subnet/port scanning.
+ *   - Hosts are strictly validated: the device must be a literal RFC 1918
+ *     private IPv4 address; the CLI must be a loopback address. Hostnames
+ *     (other than "localhost") are rejected, which removes DNS rebinding as a
+ *     way to redirect requests elsewhere.
+ *   - Only a fixed, read-only diagnostics path is requested. Users choose the
+ *     host and port, never the path, method, or body.
+ *   - Requests use `targetAddressSpace` so Chrome's Local Network Access
+ *     permission prompt is shown, `credentials: 'omit'` so no ambient cookies
+ *     are sent, `redirect: 'error'` so the target can't bounce us elsewhere,
+ *     and a short timeout.
+ *   - A pairing token (shown by the device / CLI) is sent as a bearer header.
+ *     This forces a CORS preflight, so the target must explicitly opt in to
+ *     this origin, and proves the reader controls the target. The token is kept
+ *     in memory only — never persisted or sent anywhere else.
+ *   - Responses are size-limited, parsed as JSON, and rendered as text (React
+ *     escapes it); nothing from the target is ever executed or injected as HTML.
+ */
